            {
              "category": "未宣言の前提への依存",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/test_obligation/waiver_cache_codec.rs",
              "line": 200,
              "message": "`未宣言の前提への依存`: `save` now routes every waiver-cache write through `open_fulfillment_cache_for_write_guarded`, whose non-Unix branch unconditionally returns `Unsupported`. This regresses the previous `std::fs::write` behavior on Windows, while neither the track spec nor `environment-assumptions.md` declares waiver-cache persistence Unix-only. Provide a descriptor-pinned writer for supported non-Unix targets, or formally declare and enforce this adapter's platform boundary.",
              "severity": "P1"
            }
          ],
          "hash": "rvw1:sha256:5476ffa077feb0f366da0491832476c4d6ccba318df7c1140107ee7ccc082dc6",
          "type": "final",
          "verdict": "findings_remain"
        },
        {
          "at": "2026-09-11T21:41:56Z",
          "findings": [
            {
              "category": "trusted-root violation",
              "file": "libs/infrastructure/src/test_obligation/waiver_cache_codec.rs",
              "line": 248,
              "message": "The new Windows writer remains check-then-use: `create_dir_all(parent)` mutates by pathname, then `reject_symlinks_below` checks the path, and `.open(path)` resolves the full pathname again. `FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT` protects only the final component; it does not pin intermediate directories. A junction can replace an ancestor around either check, causing directory creation or the later truncate/write outside `trusted_root`. Use component-by-component handle-relative traversal like the Unix writer, or formally make this adapter fail closed on Windows.",
              "severity": "P0"
            }
          ],
          "hash": "rvw1:sha256:81d6682541b373f4c4680bbb6add6725a08146c9479d371ba20715a15d7ed4e6",
          "type": "final",
